WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.069
Speaker 1: OK, let's jump in to audience questions
for the week, first question about guns.

00:00:04.069 --> 00:00:07.270
David, why have mass shootings skyrocketed
in the United States?

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:11.490
It is true that mass shootings have increased
in the United States.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:17.949
There is unlikely to be a significant agreement
on this issue politically right now.

00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:23.130
But to me, it seems very clear that there
are at least a few factors that together have

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:27.560
led the United States to be in the situation
that it's in now for the people that focus

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.430
only on guns.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:33.110
I actually don't think guns are the problem.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.290
They are a major problem in the United States.

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:41.630
The easy availability of firearms, combined
with the lack of a universal background check

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:48.620
program, the lack of an insurance requirement,
the ease with which you can purchase large

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:53.740
capacity magazines, I mean, there's a lot
that relates to guns, but.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:58.320
There are countries like Switzerland, and
I know it's almost cliche to even bring it

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.920
up at this point in time, there are countries
with high rates of gun ownership that do not

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:08.240
have our gun violence problem, and that is
because of something else.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.499
And I believe that it is a result of culture.

00:01:10.499 --> 00:01:14.359
And by culture, I mean gun culture in the
United States.

00:01:14.359 --> 00:01:20.770
The idea that in the United States, more than
in so many other of the rich OECD countries

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:26.450
and the United States, there's this idea where
people see firearms as a solution to problems,

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:31.079
as a solution to interpersonal problems, as
a solution to being an NSL.

00:01:31.079 --> 00:01:35.460
I mean, I know it sounds weird, but like that
that's also a factor in some of these shootings.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.069
In individual cases, it's a solution to work
problems.

00:01:39.069 --> 00:01:43.259
It's a solution to political problems in the
eyes of many, that is very different than

00:01:43.259 --> 00:01:44.399
in other countries.

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:48.539
And so when you look at things like should
we have universal background checks?

00:01:48.539 --> 00:01:54.179
Well, yeah, of course, when you look at the
2004 the federal assault weapons ban, which

00:01:54.179 --> 00:02:01.560
expired in 2004, there were there was a spike
in shootings after that expired.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.249
But there is a debate as to the degree to
which it is responsible, because there was

00:02:05.249 --> 00:02:13.170
also the sort of furthering of this wacky
right wing gun culture at that point in time

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:16.019
during the era of George W. Bush.

00:02:16.019 --> 00:02:22.110
And so it seems clear that there is a unique
set of circumstances in the United States,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.299
which is culture, which includes ignorant
people.

00:02:26.299 --> 00:02:33.459
It includes people who see guns as a solution
to problems, availability of guns.

00:02:33.459 --> 00:02:34.459
Yes.

00:02:34.459 --> 00:02:39.430
And then also, you know, lack of access to
mental health care and the fact that even

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:45.439
for people who do have access, it's often
highly stigmatized extremism.

00:02:45.439 --> 00:02:51.140
There is a lot of extremism in the United
States compared to many other countries, which

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:56.769
leads people to look for things like guns
with which to then go and do things.

00:02:56.769 --> 00:03:04.579
So my point here is not to say that it is
that that it is one issue at the exclusion

00:03:04.579 --> 00:03:10.269
of others or that we shouldn't be looking
deeply at gun safety itself.

00:03:10.269 --> 00:03:11.659
We absolutely should.

00:03:11.659 --> 00:03:16.590
But we need to understand that there is also
something special in the United States.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:23.400
A lot of these movements that in other countries
are hyper fringe, like hyper hyper fringe

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:27.840
in the United States, they are fringe, but
it's a big enough country where there's still

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.069
a lot of them.

00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:36.730
60 Minutes segment over last weekend about
the Oath Keepers, which is one of these right

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:39.609
wing militia type groups.

00:03:39.609 --> 00:03:40.609
These things exist.

00:03:40.609 --> 00:03:41.609
I get it.

00:03:41.609 --> 00:03:44.030
There's this right nationalist front in many
European countries.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.840
And, you know, Australia has examples of these
groups.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.629
Australia has very different gun laws, I think
it's important to say.

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:53.910
But there is a huge cultural component, which
if we ignore, we need to focus on the gun

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:58.080
piece because we're humiliatingly behind on
dealing with guns.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:03.049
But there's also something special, and I
don't mean in a good way about American culture

00:04:03.049 --> 00:04:52.580
that correlates and and I believe is causally
related to these shootings 

00:04:52.580 --> 00:05:15.220
as well.

